 To compute the determinant of this matrix, we can use the property of determinants and the fact that $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are the roots of the cubic equation $x^3 + px + q = 0.$ Let's expand the determinant:
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{vmatrix}
&= a\begin{vmatrix} c & a \\ a & b \end{vmatrix} - b\begin{vmatrix} b & a \\ c & b \end{vmatrix} + c\begin{vmatrix} b & c \\ c & a \end{vmatrix} \\
&= a(cb - a^2) - b(b^2 - ac) + c(ba - c^2) \\
&= abc - a^3 - b^3 - c^3 + 3abc \\
&= 4abc - (a^3 + b^3 + c^3).
\end{align*}
To simplify the expression $a^3 + b^3 + c^3,$ we can use the identity $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3abc = (a + b + c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - ac - bc).$ Since $a,$ $b,$ and $c$ are the roots of the given cubic equation, we know that $a + b + c = 0$ and $ab + ac + bc = p.$ Therefore, $a^2 + b^2 + c^2 = (a + b + c)^2 - 2(ab + ac + bc) = 0 - 2p = -2p.$ Substituting these values into the identity, we have
\begin{align*}
a^3 + b^3 + c^3 - 3abc
&= (a + b + c)(a^2 + b^2 + c^2 - ab - ac - bc) \\
&= 0(-2p - p) \\
&= 0,
\end{align*}
and thus $a^3 + b^3 + c^3 = 3abc.$ Substituting this back into our determinant expression, we get
\begin{align*}
\begin{vmatrix} a & b & c \\ b & c & a \\ c & a & b \end{vmatrix}
&= 4abc - (a^3 + b^3 + c^3) \\
&= 4abc - 3abc \\
&= abc.
\end{align*}
</think> The answer is $\boxed{abc}.$</answer>
